        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill under $200?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most people, the UREVO Strol 2E at about $170: a 40-inch track, a 4 mph walking mode and a 6.2 mph jogging mode with the handle raised. The cheapest machine we would buy is the Goplus 2.25HP 2-in-1, which one listing priced at $113.99.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you get a good treadmill for under $100?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not one we would recommend. On the day we checked, the only motorized treadmills under $100 on Amazon US were walking pads from sellers we did not recognise. The closest established brand was Goplus at $113.99. A used treadmill you can test in person is usually a better use of $100.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you run on a treadmill under $200?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              You can jog briefly on a 2-in-1 pad, but not run regularly. Belts at this price are 35 to 40 inches long, against 55 to 60 inches for running, and the motors are designed around walking. Treat the jogging mode as short intervals within a walk.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a walking pad or a manual treadmill better for under $200?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A walking pad if you want long, steady walks, especially at a desk. A manual treadmill if you want a traditional frame with handrails, no outlet, or a hard incline workout in a short time. Manual belts feel like uphill walking all the time, which many people find tiring.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long does a treadmill under $200 last?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on use and care more than the price. Staying well under the weight limit, lubricating the belt on schedule, keeping it clean and using it mostly at walking pace all help. Warranties at this price are short, often a year or less, so read the terms on the listing you buy from.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why do prices on cheap treadmills change so much?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Budget pads are sold under several listings and colours with frequent coupons and sales, so the same machine can be $50 apart on two listings on the same day. Compare every listing for the model and set a price alert rather than buying the first result.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

